{
 "ep": 9,
 "total": 140,
 "title": "PostgreSQL Data Types You Should Know",
 "phase": "Foundations",
 "lines": [
  {
   "say": "Choosing the wrong data type can hurt you later.",
   "cap": "The wrong *data* *type* can *hurt* later."
  },
  {
   "say": "This is episode nine of the PostgreSQL DBA series. Today: the data types a DBA should know.",
   "cap": "Episode 9: the *data* *types* to know."
  },
  {
   "say": "A column's type decides the allowed values, the storage size, and which operations work.",
   "cap": "A type decides *values,* *size* and *operations.*",
   "cue": "operations"
  },
  {
   "say": "Changing it later normally rewrites the whole table and its indexes. So choose well.",
   "cap": "Changing it later *rewrites* the table.",
   "cue": "rewrites"
  },
  {
   "say": "Integers first. smallint is two bytes, integer four, bigint eight.",
   "cap": "*smallint* 2, *integer* 4, *bigint* 8 bytes.",
   "cue": "bigint"
  },
  {
   "say": "The docs call integer the common choice. Use bigint when its range is not enough.",
   "cap": "*integer* is the common choice. *bigint* for more range.",
   "cue": "bigint"
  },
  {
   "say": "Past about two point one billion, integer fails: integer out of range. bigint handles it.",
   "cap": "Past 2,147,483,647: *integer* *out* *of* *range.*",
   "cue": "bigint"
  },
  {
   "say": "For money, use numeric: it is exact. real and double precision are inexact.",
   "cap": "Money: *numeric* is exact. Floats are *inexact.*",
   "cue": "real"
  },
  {
   "say": "Zero point one plus zero point two as double precision is not exactly zero point three. With numeric, it is.",
   "cap": "*float8:* 0.30000000000000004. *numeric:* 0.3.",
   "cue": "numeric"
  },
  {
   "say": "For strings, text and varchar perform the same in PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "*text* and *varchar:* same performance."
  },
  {
   "say": "char of n pads with spaces, and is usually the slowest.",
   "cap": "*char(n)* pads with spaces: usually *slowest.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "varchar of n adds a length check. Too long, and you get: value too long for type character varying.",
   "cap": "Too long: *value* *too* *long* for type varchar.",
   "cue": "value"
  },
  {
   "say": "boolean is one byte: true, false, or null for unknown.",
   "cap": "*boolean:* 1 byte. true, false or *null.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "For time, a common practice is timestamptz. It is stored as UTC, and shown in your session's time zone.",
   "cap": "*timestamptz* is stored as *UTC.*",
   "cue": "shown"
  },
  {
   "say": "Here, seven thirty UTC shows as one PM in Asia Kolkata.",
   "cap": "07:30 UTC shows as *13:00 +05:30.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "A uuid is sixteen bytes. gen_random_uuid makes a random one.",
   "cap": "*uuid:* 16 bytes. *gen_random_uuid()* = random v4.",
   "cue": "gen_random_uuid"
  },
  {
   "say": "PostgreSQL eighteen adds uuidv7, which is time-ordered.",
   "cap": "PostgreSQL 18 adds *uuidv7():* time-ordered."
  },
  {
   "say": "The docs say most apps should prefer jsonb. It is stored as binary, and much faster to process.",
   "cap": "Prefer *jsonb:* binary and *faster* to process.",
   "cue": "binary"
  },
  {
   "say": "jsonb can also use GIN indexes. If a key appears twice, jsonb keeps only the last value.",
   "cap": "*jsonb* keeps the *last* duplicate key.",
   "cue": "twice"
  },
  {
   "say": "Arrays hold a list in one column, numbered from one.",
   "cap": "Arrays are numbered from *1.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "But the docs warn: arrays are not sets. If you search inside them, a separate table often scales better.",
   "cap": "Arrays are *not* *sets.* Consider a *table.*",
   "cue": "separate"
  },
  {
   "say": "DBA takeaway: typlen in pg_type shows the size of fixed-length types.",
   "cap": "*pg_type.typlen* = size in bytes."
  },
  {
   "say": "One byte for boolean, four for integer, eight for bigint, sixteen for uuid.",
   "cap": "bool *1,* int4 *4,* int8 *8,* uuid *16.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "Common mistake: storing dates or numbers as text. You lose validation, correct sorting, and date math.",
   "cap": "*Never* store dates or numbers as *text.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "Recap: integers for counts, numeric for money, text for strings, timestamptz for time, jsonb for JSON.",
   "cap": "Recap: *bigint,* *numeric,* *text,* *timestamptz,* *jsonb.*",
   "cue": "timestamptz"
  },
  {
   "say": "Next up: psql, the essential PostgreSQL command-line tool. Follow so you don't miss it, and send this to a friend who's learning PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "Next up: *psql.* *Follow* and share."
  }
 ],
